#!/bin/sh
# PID 1 for the terminal-fun container. Its only job is to decide which uid the backend runs
# as — and to guarantee it is never root.
#
# The image has to boot under BOTH compose topologies, because the uid split is switched on in
# deploy/docker-compose.yml and an image that only starts under the new one takes the rail down
# the moment it is pulled ahead of the compose change.
#
#   A. Container starts NON-ROOT — today's compose: no `user:` key, so the image's own
#      `USER funuser` applies. Nothing to drop, nothing to grant. The backend runs as funuser
#      and, holding no CAP_SETUID, spawns games under its own uid: exactly the behaviour this
#      rail shipped with. pty_session.split_uid_active() detects this and does not wrap.
#
#   B. Container starts as ROOT with the two drop-privilege capabilities granted:
#          user: "0:0"
#          cap_drop: [ALL]          # stays
#          cap_add:  [SETUID, SETGID]
#          security_opt: ["no-new-privileges:true"]   # stays
#      We drop to funuser here and keep those two caps AMBIENT, which is the only way the
#      backend can later put a game on gameuser. It has to happen here rather than via the
#      image's USER because Docker never populates the ambient set, so capabilities granted to
#      a container whose USER is non-root simply do not reach the process.
#
# no-new-privileges does not interfere: it blocks privilege GAIN through execve (setuid bits,
# file capabilities). setpriv changes uid with setresuid(2), a capability check, not an exec.
#
# Root must never reach uvicorn. If we are uid 0 and cannot drop, that is a misconfiguration —
# say which one, and exit rather than "helpfully" serving the rail as root.
set -eu

if [ "$(id -u)" -ne 0 ]; then
    exec "$@"
fi

DROP="--reuid=funuser --regid=funuser --init-groups
      --inh-caps=+setuid,+setgid --ambient-caps=+setuid,+setgid"

# Probe before committing: `setpriv ... true` costs one fork at boot and turns an opaque
# "container exited 1" into the message below.
# shellcheck disable=SC2086
if setpriv $DROP true 2>/dev/null; then
    # shellcheck disable=SC2086
    exec setpriv $DROP -- "$@"
fi

cat >&2 <<'MSG'
terminal-fun: started as uid 0 but cannot drop privileges — CAP_SETUID/CAP_SETGID are not
granted, so setpriv(1) fails. Refusing to run the backend as root.

Fix the compose service one of two ways:
  * remove `user: "0:0"`  -> the image's own USER funuser applies and games run as the
    backend's uid (the pre-split behaviour: correct, just not hardened); or
  * keep it and add, alongside the existing `cap_drop: [ALL]`:
        cap_add: [SETUID, SETGID]
    -> the backend runs as funuser and games run as gameuser, which is the point.
MSG
exit 1
